<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gurdwara Darbar Sahib Kartarpur</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kartarpur Sahib</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gurdwara Darbar Sahib Kartarpur is a gurdwara in Kartarpur, in the Narowal District of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>, beside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ravi">Ravi</a> River. It stands on the site where, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> tradition, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guru-nanak">Guru Nanak</a> settled and gathered the Sikh community after his journeys, living there for 18 years until his death in 1539. The main shrine was built in 1925 with funds from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharaja">Maharaja</a> of Patiala, repaired in 1995 and fully restored in 2004. A new courtyard, museum and library were added in November 2018. The shrine lies near the India-Pakistan border, and the Kartarpur Corridor, opened on 9 November 2019, gave Indian pilgrims visa-free access.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gurdwara-darbar-sahib-kartarpur.jpg" alt="Gurdwara Darbar Sahib Kartarpur" width="330" height="366" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Xubayr Mayo · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kartarpur_Guru_Nanak.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> tradition <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guru-nanak">Guru Nanak</a> spent his last years at Kartarpur, where he established a community, farmed, and set up a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/langar-sikhism">langar</a>, or community kitchen. There he gave the principles known as Kirat Karo, Naam Japo and Vand Chako: to work honestly for a livelihood, to remember God, and to share one's earnings with others.</p><p>Tradition also holds that a dispute followed his death between local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a> and Muslims, the Muslims regarding him as their pir and wishing to bury him and the Hindus, who claimed him as their guru, preferring cremation. In one popular legend his body turned into flowers, which the two communities divided. In another telling the dispute was over his shawl, which was divided, the Muslims burying their part and the Hindus cremating theirs.</p><p>Because the Ravi River repeatedly flooded the cremation site, his son Baba Sri Chand moved the ashes to a safer place, now called Dera Baba Nanak. The main shrine building was put up in 1925 at a cost of Rs. 1,35,600, given by Sardar Bhupindar Singh, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharaja">Maharaja</a> of Patiala. The Government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> repaired it in 1995 and restored it fully in 2004. The site also holds a well of small red bricks, about 500 years old and believed to date from Guru Nanak's lifetime.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The gurdwara stands at Kartarpur in the Shakargarh Tehsil of Narowal District, beside the Ravi River and about 3 kilometres from the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. It can be seen from the Indian side, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikhs</a> gather on bluffs, especially on Gurpurab, the birth anniversary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guru-nanak">Guru Nanak</a>, to obtain darshan, or sacred viewing, of the shrine. It is counted among the holiest sites in Sikhism.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Visa-free access for Indian pilgrims was long a demand of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> community. The idea was raised during <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atal-bihari-vajpayee">Atal Bihari Vajpayee</a>'s bus journey to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a> in 1999, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pervez-musharraf">Pervez Musharraf</a> approved it in 2000. In May 2017 an Indian parliamentary committee said no corridor would be built, and that binoculars might be placed at Dera Baba Nanak instead.</p><p>In September 2018 the Government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> decided to open a corridor for up to 5,000 Indian Sikhs a day, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/government-of-india">Government of India</a> approved a road from Dera Baba Nanak to the border. The Kartarpur Corridor opened on 9 November 2019, days before the 550th birth anniversary of Guru Nanak. Narendra Modi and Imran Khan took part in the opening, and the first jatha of more than 550 pilgrims, led by Giani Harpreet Singh, crossed into Pakistan.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gurdwara Darbar Sahib Kartarpur” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
